.Sh DESCRIPTION
These functions deliver the structured event log that a worker produces
as it builds and runs a dataflow.
This is the machine-readable log that the diagnostics tools consume to
reconstruct the operator graph and the timing profile; it is distinct
from the human-readable diagnostic sink installed by
.Xr dtfl_set_log_hook 3 .
.Pp
A worker emits events on named streams:
.Bl -tag -width "differential/arrange" -offset indent
.It Dq timely
operator and channel creation, scheduling, messages, and parking
.It Dq differential/arrange
arrangement batch, merge, drop and trace-sharing events
.It Dq communication
message send and receive, and communication-thread state.
These come from the network send and receive threads of a cluster
.Pq Dv DTFL_COMM_CLUSTER
and are delivered only to a subscription made with
.Fn dtfl_config_log_subscribe ;
the batch's worker index is the process index.
.El
On the wire each stream is the bincode-1 fixint little-endian encoding
of the corresponding Rust 0.31 event enum;
a subscriber receives the events already decoded, so a C caller never
touches the wire bytes.
.Pp
.Fn dtfl_worker_log_subscribe
registers
.Fa fn
for
.Fa stream
on a worker the caller owns, such as one from
.Xr dtfl_worker_new_standalone 3 .
.Fn dtfl_config_log_subscribe
records a subscription on a configuration; every worker that
.Xr dtfl_execute 3
starts installs it as it comes up.
A second subscribe of the same stream replaces the first.
.Fa ctx_free ,
if not NULL, releases
.Fa ctx :
for the worker form, when the subscriber is replaced or the worker is
dropped; for the configuration form, once, when the configuration is
freed.
.Pp
A subscriber is called on the worker thread with a batch of events as
they are flushed, once per worker step and once more, with an empty
batch, when the worker shuts down.
A batch with
.Fn dtfl_log_batch_len
of zero is such a flush notification.
The batch and every pointer an accessor returns are valid only for the
duration of the subscriber call; a subscriber that keeps data copies it.
.Pp
.Fn dtfl_log_batch_time
is the batch capability time and
.Fn dtfl_log_event_time
each event's time, both in nanoseconds since the worker started.
.Fn dtfl_log_batch_worker
is the worker index and
.Fn dtfl_log_batch_stream
the stream name.
.Pp
.Fn dtfl_log_event_kind_at
returns the kind of event
.Fa i ,
one of the
.Vt dtfl_log_event_kind
values in
.In dtfl/log.h ,
which union the Rust TimelyEvent, DifferentialEvent and
CommunicationEvent variants.
The field accessors read the fields of the event whose kind they name;
reading an accessor that does not match the event's kind returns a zero
value, so a subscriber dispatches on
.Fn dtfl_log_event_kind_at
first.
The accessors shown above are the ones a graph or profile tool needs:
.Fn dtfl_log_operates_id ,
.Fn dtfl_log_operates_addr
and
.Fn dtfl_log_operates_name
give an operator's identifier, its nested-scope address, and its name;
.Fn dtfl_log_channels_id ,
.Fn dtfl_log_channels_source ,
.Fn dtfl_log_channels_target
and
.Fn dtfl_log_channels_typ
give a channel's identifier, its endpoints as (operator, port) pairs,
and its data type; and
.Fn dtfl_log_schedule_id
with
.Fn dtfl_log_schedule_start
give the operator scheduled and whether the event is its start or its
stop.
.In dtfl/log.h
declares the remaining accessors for messages, parking, the
differential events and the communication events.
.Sh RETURN VALUES
.Fn dtfl_worker_log_subscribe
and
.Fn dtfl_config_log_subscribe
return
.Dv DTFL_OK ,
.Dv DTFL_EINVAL
for a NULL argument, or
.Dv DTFL_ESTATE
if the worker has no log registry.
The accessors do not fail; an out-of-range index or a kind mismatch
returns a zero value.
.Sh EXAMPLES
Build a dataflow graph from the
.Dq timely
stream:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
static void
on_batch(const dtfl_log_batch *b, void *ctx)
{
	size_t i, n = dtfl_log_batch_len(b);

	for (i = 0; i < n; i++) {
		if (dtfl_log_event_kind_at(b, i) == DTFL_LOG_OPERATES) {
			size_t na;
			const size_t *addr =
			    dtfl_log_operates_addr(b, i, &na);

			add_operator(ctx, dtfl_log_operates_id(b, i), addr, na,
			    dtfl_log_operates_name(b, i));
		}
	}
}

dtfl_config_log_subscribe(cfg, "timely", on_batch, graph, NULL);
dtfl_execute(cfg, worker_main, app, NULL);
.Ed
.Sh THREAD SAFETY
A subscriber runs on the worker thread that logs to it, except that a
.Dq communication
subscriber runs on the cluster's network threads, one call at a time
per thread but concurrently across them.
With more than one worker or network thread the same
.Fa ctx
is shared across worker and network threads, as the
.Fa ctx
of
.Xr dtfl_execute 3
is; a subscriber that writes shared state must synchronize it itself.
A slow
.Dq communication
subscriber delays the network thread that calls it, and so the sends
or receives that thread serves.
.Pp
The configuration and every subscriber
.Fa ctx
must stay valid until
.Xr dtfl_guards_join 3
returns: when
.Xr dtfl_execute 3
returns early with guards, the worker and network threads keep using
them.
.Pp
Each network thread's logger measures event times from when that thread
started, so
.Dq communication
times are comparable within one thread's events but not across threads
or with the worker streams.
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr dtfl_execute 3 ,
.Xr dtfl_set_log_hook 3 ,
.Xr dtfl_worker_dataflow 3 ,
.Xr dtfl 7
.Sh HISTORY
This is the C form of enabling a timely or differential log stream with
upstream's
.Ql worker.log_register().insert_logger ,
whose action receives decoded batches instead of a serialized
.Vt EventWriter
stream.
It replaces the per-record worker identifier that timely dropped in 0.16
with the worker index on the batch.
